# Snowflake Monorepo Workflow

## Worktree-based development
This repo uses bind-mount worktrees for parallel development. ALWAYS use the `worktree` CLI (a shell script at ~/.local/bin/worktree, on PATH):

- `worktree create <name> [base]` — new worktree branch
- `worktree activate <name>` — bind-mount for build/test
- `worktree deactivate` — restore base repo
- `worktree status` — check what's active
- `worktree list` — list all worktrees
- `worktree remove <name>` — remove a worktree
- `worktree build <name> <bazel-args...>` — activate + build + deactivate

## Rules
- Never modify /home/repo/snowflake-base directly for feature work
- Create a worktree for each task/branch
- Activate before building, deactivate when done
- The mount point /home/repo/snowflake is where builds happen
- Each worktree has a paired ~/sf directory (build artifacts and logs)
  - ~/sf-base — default artifacts, ~/sf-worktrees/<name> — per-worktree artifacts
  - `activate` and `deactivate` swap both mounts together

## Testing with Snowfort
- For Snowfort tests, read and follow the skills under `/home/repo/snowflake/Snowfort/.claude/`
- Use `snowsql -c reg` to run SQL directly against Snowflake after deploying changes

## Deployment
- Use `sf sut redeploy` to build and deploy local changes (builds both GS and XP)
- Only use full DPO/parameter deploys if making fundamental changes to DPO or parameters
- Always activate the worktree before deploying

## Build Verification
- ALWAYS run `sf sut build` to verify changes compile successfully before committing
- The worktree MUST be activated before running `sf sut build` (it builds from /home/repo/snowflake)
- Fix any build errors before proceeding with commit/push

## Code Formatting
- ALWAYS run `snow format` on modified files before committing
- This applies to all languages in the monorepo (Java, Python, SQL, etc.)
- Format first, then activate worktree, then verify build, then commit

## Parallel Agents
- Use up to 5 parallel agents for all work (research, implementation, testing)
- Prefer parallel execution whenever tasks are independent
